{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788310895,"nonce":"7EgtKirLiSikbR4co1ILWVRbPfA=","title":"**Filed in haste, mid-run (m1-20260901, tick 1023). Loose on purpose — it needs an agent to refine it against the source before anyone builds it.** Evan's framing, and the shape below is his.","message":"Five forced orders were issued to two pawns while the game was PAUSED, in five separate calls:\n\n```\nequip {pawn:323, thing:2514}   # bolt-action rifle  -\u003e job 381, order_effect \"started\"\nequip {pawn:310, thing:2515}   # revolver           -\u003e job 382, order_effect \"started\"\nwear  {pawn:323, thing:2518}   # flak vest          -\u003e job 383, order_effect \"started\"\nwear  {pawn:323, thing:2519}   # flak helmet        -\u003e job 384, order_effect \"started\"\nwear  {pawn:323, thing:2517}   # flak pants         -\u003e job 385, order_effect \"started\"\n```\n\nEvery one returned `ok:true` with an `accepted` row naming the job it started, `player_forced:true`, `queue:false`, `queue_depth:0`. All four of Jimmy's orders were truthful and all four were **the same slot**: 383 replaced 381, 384 replaced 383, 385 replaced 384. Jimmy ended up wearing flak pants and holding nothing. Nothing in any of the four envelopes said a forced job had just been thrown away.\n\nZero ticks had run between them, so no order could possibly have completed. The mistake is only visible by diffing `queue_depth` and `order_effect` across four calls that each individually read like a success.\n\n\n\n`queue:true` exists and fixes the collision — re-issuing the three with it produced `order_effect:\"queued\"` and `queue_depth` 1, 2, 3, which is the correct end state. But the default silently discards a player-forced job, and the caller learns nothing.\n\nThe verbs already have the vocabulary for this. `4087644` shipped an `already-doing-it` gate, and `7382bdd` shipped `ignored_args` — the pattern of \"the verb ran, and here is the thing you probably did not mean\" is established. What is missing is: **a forced order aimed at a pawn who already holds a `player_forced` job is a collision, and the envelope must name the job it displaced** — its `job_id`, its `job_def`, and the fact that it was cancelled rather than completed. Whether that is a refusal (like `RefuseStray`) or a loud non-fatal field (like `ignored_args`) is the design question. Evan's reading is that it wants at minimum a warning, so that the agent catches the need to either pass `queue:true` or let the first order finish.\n\nThe asymmetry worth pinning while doing it: displacing an *autonomous* job is the whole point of a forced order (`rescue` FORCES through `TryTakeOrderedJob` and interrupts `LayDown` — that is `casualty-halt`'s sharpest finding and must not regress). Displacing a *player-forced* job is the caller contradicting themselves. Only the second is the collision.\n\n\nThe deeper half. Today, \"did the order land\" is answerable only by advancing some number of ticks and re-reading `pawn {sections:[\"state\"]}`. That makes every forced order a poll, and polling is exactly the failure mode `read-every-return-or-lose-a-colonist` was written about — a narrowed read on a loop.\n\n`advance` already solves this shape for construction: `--until.layout ly-1` halts when every element of that transaction is built or cancelled, and publishes `built`/`cancelled`/`unresolved` plus `unresolved_items` with each outstanding element's state. **A forced order wants the same treatment** — some form of `--until.order \u003cjob_id\u003e` / `--until.orders` that halts when the queued forced jobs for a pawn (or for the colony) have drained, and reports per-job `completed` / `cancelled` / `still-queued` with the game's own reason for a cancellation.\n\nWithout it the agent cannot tell these apart: the order completed; the order was interrupted by a mental break or a raid; the pawn could not reach the item; the item was hauled away by someone else first. All four look identical to \"advance 2000 ticks and read the state again\", and only one of them is fine.\n\n\nThe immediate cost here was small — the queue was rebuilt in three calls and no tick had run. But the same shape is how a colonist ends up unarmed at a raid letter while the transcript shows five green `ok:true` equip calls, which is one variant of M1's `weapons-have-no-alert` death.\n\n\n1. A forced-order verb (`equip`, `wear`, `drop`, `carry`, `consume`, `move-to`, `attack`, `rescue`, `tend`, `prioritize` — the set wants confirming against `PawnOrderVerbs` / `PawnActs`) aimed at a pawn already holding a `player_forced` job, without `queue:true`, reports the displacement: the displaced `job_id`, `job_def` and label, in a named envelope field, on the same call. A bench transcript shows two such calls back to back and the second naming the first's job.\n2. Displacing a non-forced job stays silent and stays working. A regression test proves `rescue` still interrupts `LayDown` with no new warning and no refusal — `casualty-halt` depends on it.\n3. `advance` gains a halt matcher for forced-order completion, in the shape `--until.layout` already uses: it halts when the named order (or the pawn's forced queue) drains, and the result distinguishes `completed` from `cancelled` and carries the game's own cancellation reason. `unresolved_items`' analogue for orders is what makes \"the pawn could not reach it\" different from \"someone else took it\".\n4. `accept/\u003cid\u003e-order-completion.py` on a bench: issue two colliding forced orders paused, assert the warning; issue a queued pair, advance on the new matcher, assert it halts on drain rather than on the timeout, and assert the per-job verdicts.\n5. Whatever lands is written up in `playbook/` or a checklist so the act-keyed moment (\"after any forced order\") has a trigger, since a lesson no checklist cites is half-landed.\n\n\n- Refusal or warning for A? A refusal is safer and matches `RefuseStray`'s three dangerous-default verbs; a warning keeps `ok:true` callers working. Evan said \"without a warning\" — so a warning is the floor, not necessarily the ceiling.\n- Does the completion matcher key on a `job_id`, on a pawn, or on a run-scoped order tag? `job_id` is what the envelopes already publish, which argues for it, but a queued job's id is assigned at enqueue time and the queue can be flushed.\n- Is `queue_depth:0` with `order_effect:\"started\"` enough of a signal to detect this client-side in the meantime? It is — the run's wrapper could warn on it — but that puts game semantics in the client, which spec 1.4 forbids.","files":null}]}